[Music]  the items we see on store shelves don't  show up there by chance  retailers decide what will occupy that  precious space  now a growing movement is trying to  ensure that black owned businesses have  an opportunity  to sell their goods in proportion with  the black population  it's called the 15 percent pledge and i  spoke with founder aurora james about  why she believes disrupting business as  usual  was the only option i was so  fed up as you know a woman and a woman  of color of being disappointed by the  choices that  people or companies were making but i  also realized that i  failed in a lot of ways to really ask  for what i  needed it was out of frustration that  fashion designer aurora james decided  she needed to be a part of the solution  [Applause]  in the aftermath of the killing of  george floyd  she took to instagram demanding major  retailers commit  15 of their shelf space to black owned  brands  i was having conversations with my  friends about all these corporations and  you know the donations that they were  making and i was really like no guys  like we need real change you know black  businesses are dying out  according to a report conducted last  june the national bureau of economic  research found that 41 percent of black  owned businesses have closed due to the  pandemic  compare that to less than 17 percent of  white-owned businesses  james founded her own luxury accessory  brand  brother vellis in 2013 and knows  firsthand the barriers black  entrepreneurs face  i really did that without ever having  even a loan from my  bank or a company credit card it was  always one of those things where i was  like oh you know it's weird even first  round of ppp  like didn't get it you didn't get it no  the bank said no to you i was able to  like get  get you know loans from various people  but to be honest they weren't healthy  loans that were helpful for my  businesses  they weren't loans that like any of my  white male colleagues in the industry  would have ever ever ever  ever ever had to get along with  committing shelf space  companies are asked to conduct an  internal audit of their current spending  power and contracts  allocated to black businesses i'm  curious  what you tell companies are going to get  out of it on the back end  when we look at consumer trends right  now we're also seeing that  people want to shop places that they  believe in  and they're not willing to just spend  money anywhere anymore  because we have fought hard for every  single dollar that we make right now as  americans  i'm wearing makeup and i'm totally  comfortable sephora was the very first  company to commit  we were very excited i'll tell you  within within minutes of  seeing the pledge artemis patrick is  executive vice president  and global chief merchandising officer  for sephora  within minutes of talking to aurora it  became very clear  that we were aligned that this is really  about a long-term plan  that builds a platform for black-owned  and black-founded  beauty prestige brands to grow and  succeed how specifically are you and  aurora working together  to tackle this big overall it absolutely  should be  that the the people who are creating the  brands should also be a representation  of the people who are buying the brand  sephora evolved an already existing  program  dedicated to women founders and created  a new initiative  that focuses solely on building black  indigenous  and people of color founded beauty  brands  it was very emotional marie claudio  amusa  and alice lynn glover created skincare  line edom  and are among the first group of women  selected so alice walk me through it  what is the process  like it was like winning the lottery for  me we then interviewed with the sephora  team  and i really have to give them credit  because i think when we're explaining  our product a lot of people struggle to  understand  exactly what we're talking about but  they themselves had a diverse panel of  interviewers  and so they really seemed to understand  what we were building and why it was  important  they met at google bonding over their  shared immigrant background  and being one of the few women in the  tech company  the two always discuss beauty but the  idea to start a brand  came years before it was back in 2014  and this very famous black hollywood  actress  was the face of a well-known brand and  they had just launched a fun  foundation product and i was like oh my  god someone that looks like me  i need to buy this product and i get to  the counter and i ask for my [ __ ] and  i'm like i want whatever she has  and the salesperson looks at me and he  says  oh we don't have your shade here so  the shades available they make the shade  they just didn't bother to put your  color in the store  yeah it's exactly that and i was like  okay i really need to do something in  this industry because if that's what i'm  seeing  i'm sure there are other pockets out  there where we can bring  change and more diversity for james it's  about  the pledge making a difference when we  are on the other side  what will society look like when you've  met these goals  when we talk about supporting small  businesses and we talk about supporting  small black-owned businesses in this  country what that really means is  supporting underprivileged communities  that need it the most  and it's important for everyone to  understand that the 15  pledge isn't just about positively  impacting black people  it's about positively impacting the  country at whole  and that's really the bottom line a  rising tide lifts all boats inc magazine  says that  minority women now control nearly half  of  all women run businesses women are the  backbone  entrepreneurs of you know this country  so you got to make sure that you know  they get a fighting chance and  even the playing field

